# Calendars, clocks and astronomy, one stanza per program.

shot    cal
cap     A month, one line a week.  -m and -y give the month and year; -h
cap     marks the holidays kept in SYS, and anything added to SYS/birthdays
cap     is included with them.
try     cal -m=9 -y=2026 -h
run     cal -m=9 -y=2026 -h
wait    4

shot    calen
size    90 160
cap     A month as a diary page, ruled for appointments, with the previous
cap     and next months as small calendars in the corners.  It reads the
cap     month, the year and how many months to print from its input.
try     echo 9 2026 1 | calen
os9     echo 9 2026 1 ! calen
run     echo 9 2026 1 | calen
wait    100
snap

shot    calender
size    58 100
cap     A whole year at once, in German, with the German public holidays
cap     listed under it.  It asks `Fuer welches Jahr?' -- for which year --
cap     and then asks again for the next; RETURN there ends it.
try     calender
run     calender
wait    2
send    2026\r
wait    6
snap
send    \r
wait    2

shot    digclk
cap     A clock in block digits, with the machine's name above it and the
cap     full date and time below.  By default it redraws once a minute;
cap     give it a refresh rate in seconds to update faster.  At Microware's
cap     own shell it can't find its terminal type and won't draw; at bash
cap     or ksh it works as shown.
try     digclk
run     digclk
wait    5
snap
wait    1

shot    gcl
cap     A grand digital clock: the time drawn large across the terminal,
cap     redrawn as it runs.  -n says how many seconds to run; without it
cap     it runs until stopped.
try     gcl -n=6
run     gcl -n=6
wait    4
snap
wait    4

shot    ephem
cap     A live panel from the SYS configuration and star database: date,
cap     sidereal time and site at the top, and below it each body's
cap     position -- right ascension, declination, azimuth, altitude and
cap     more.  It keeps recomputing as it runs, and without a coprocessor
cap     the outer planets take a while to fill in.  RETURN passes the
cap     opening page; control-D quits.
try     ephem -c ephem.cfg -d ephem.db
run     builtin cd /dd/SYS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     ephem -c ephem.cfg -d ephem.db
wait    8
send    \r
wait    10
snap
quit    \004

shot    ephem881
cap     The same ephemeris built for a 68881 floating-point coprocessor:
cap     with hardware floating point the whole table fills in at once,
cap     Mars through Pluto included.
try     ephem881 -c ephem.cfg -d ephem.db
run     builtin cd /dd/SYS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     ephem881 -c ephem.cfg -d ephem.db
wait    8
send    \r
wait    10
snap
quit    \004

shot    lunisolar
cap     The phase of the moon in one line.  Given a year and a time zone
cap     it writes a whole lunisolar calendar as LaTeX instead: the
cap     opening of it is below, one line a day, each one placed by that
cap     day's phase of the moon.
try     lunisolar
run     lunisolar
wait    6
run     lunisolar 2026 EST | head -n 14
wait    6

shot    nasa
cap     Converts NASA two-line orbital elements, the form satellite
cap     elements are published in, into the kepler.dat that `orbit'
cap     reads.  It takes nasa.dat from the current directory and writes
cap     kepler.dat beside it; this is one satellite in and out.
try     nasa
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/CAL > /nil 2>/nil; printf "ISS (ZARYA)\r1 25544U 98067A   26246.50000000  .00016717  00000-0  10270-3 0  9005\r2 25544  51.6416 247.4627 0006703 130.5360 325.0288 15.72125391563537\r" > /dd/tmp/CAL/nasa.dat
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/CAL
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat nasa.dat
wait    2
run     nasa
wait    8
run     cat kepler.dat
wait    4

shot    orbit
size    50 80
cap     A satellite tracker: AO-13 over Pittsburgh through a morning in
cap     March 1990, ten minutes a line -- azimuth and elevation, doppler
cap     shift, range, height, the point beneath the satellite and the
cap     transponder mode it is in.  It reads kepler.dat, mode.dat and a
cap     site file by name from the current directory, and DOC/orbit holds
cap     them, so it is run from there.
try     orbit
run     builtin cd /dd/DOC/orbit
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     orbit
wait    10
send    d\r
wait    4
send    pgh\r
wait    4
send    3 10 90\r
wait    4
send    0\r
wait    4
send    1\r
wait    4
send    10\r
wait    4
send    \r
wait    20

shot    qt
cap     The time the way a person says it: `just gone ten past four',
cap     `nearly a quarter past'.  It says whatever the clock says, and
cap     near the hour or the quarter hour it chimes too.
try     qt
run     qt
wait    5

shot    setimex
cap     Checks the system clock against a hardware time source and
cap     corrects it if it drifted.  Run bare, it just reports the time
cap     it found.
try     setimex
run     setimex
wait    8

shot    today
cap     The date, the time and the moon's phase, in a sentence.
try     today
run     today
wait    6

shot    greg
cap     A Julian day number to a Gregorian date.  Its count runs one day
cap     ahead of the astronomers' convention: 2440588, which they call
cap     the first of January 1970, comes out as the second.
try     greg 2461281
run     greg 2461281
wait    6
run     greg 2440588
wait    6

shot    almanac
size    24 80
cap     Where the Sun, Moon and the eight planets stood at a given moment:
cap     right ascension and declination, the apparent diameter of each, and
cap     its distance in astronomical units.  Give it a longitude and latitude
cap     as well and it adds azimuth and altitude for an observer standing
cap     there.  Its constants were taken from the 1990 Astronomical Almanac,
cap     so the further a date lies from then the softer the accuracy.
try     almanac 0101.1995 12.0000
run     clear
wait    1
run     almanac 0101.1995 12.0000
wait    8
